# Mutation: `degraded_reasons_at` loses the arm for a node that decides kills
# and has never once been able to send one.
#
# Must fail: `a_kill_this_kernel_refuses_is_degraded_and_named`.
#
# This is the defect the reason was built for, restored. Every other path in
# `react` that does not signal returns a named refusal; the one path that *is*
# the signal being attempted and failing incremented `respond_failed` and
# stopped. A respond DaemonSet whose `capabilities.add` lost `KILL` gets EPERM
# from every kill(2), `respond_kill_total` stays 0, `executed=false` rides the
# export with the errno on it, and with this patch applied nothing in the agent
# is Degraded.
#
# What makes it worth a patch of its own is that no neighbouring signal can
# stand in. The guard *succeeded* on the step before the syscall, so
# `respond_target_confirmed` is non-zero and `TARGET_NEVER_PROVEN` is cleared
# for the life of the process by the very step that precedes the failure;
# `TARGET_CHECK_UNPROVABLE` is about a guard that could not be built, and this
# one was. Mutation 02 — a `SignalResponder` claiming a kill it never sent — is
# the exact inverse of this defect and cannot see it either. So the join has to
# carry a kill this kernel really refuses, which is what the test above does
# with a tgid one past `pid_max`.
#
# Measured on Linux 6.18.44: `FAILED. 5 passed; 1 failed`, and the failing
# assertion prints the reasons the node did give — `no kernel attach` and
# `container map not ready`, neither of which is about the signal.

diff --git a/crates/ferrum-agent/src/lib.rs b/crates/ferrum-agent/src/lib.rs
index c23007b..74c2703 100644
--- a/crates/ferrum-agent/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/ferrum-agent/src/lib.rs
@@ -840,11 +840,6 @@ impl Agent {
             // beside `TARGET_CHECK_UNPROVABLE` anyway, because a guard that
             // cannot run refuses before anything is signalled and
             // `respond_failed` then cannot move at all.
-            if self.respond_kill.load(Ordering::Relaxed) == 0
-                && self.respond_failed.load(Ordering::Relaxed) >= RESPOND_SIGNAL_FAILING_MIN
-            {
-                out.push(RESPOND_SIGNAL_FAILING.to_string());
-            }
         }
         out
     }
